A Moth project is a directory tree rooted at the directory selected by the command. That directory is both the build root and the source root. There is no implicit `src/` entry directory, and a nested `config.moth` never applies to an enclosing build.

### Starter shape

The optional project config uses ordinary named compile-time records:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    project #= (
        name = "my_site",
    )

    html #= ()
]

The `project` record and its `name` field are optional. An omitted name uses the selected directory name. The active builder section follows its own required or optional presence rule. HTML currently requires `html #= ()` even when every field uses its default.

A configurable value uses the ordinary typed input spelling until the queued `$config` marker lands. `project` and `html` stay ordinary record values. No `$project`, `$builder` or `$html_builder` directive exists.

### Project root contents

[codeblock, $code("text"):
    website/
    ├── config.moth           # optional
    ├── @entry.moth
    ├── @package.moth         # optional external package surface
    ├── shared/
    │   └── @package.moth
    ├── pages/
    │   └── @mod.moth
    ├── _dev/
    │   └── .moth_manifest
    ├── _release/
    │   └── .moth_manifest
    └── .gitignore
]

### Directory roles

- The selected directory is the only build root and the top of the discoverable source tree.
- `config.moth` is optional. When present, only the one at the selected root applies. It is not module source.
- A directory named `src/` is ordinary source content. There is no configured entry directory or `entry_root`.
- `_dev/` and `_release/` are hidden generated output folders, not source folders. The build system manages them through manifests.
- `.moth_manifest` files are build-system metadata used for safe stale-output cleanup. They are not source.
- A directory component below the selected root whose name starts with `_` is excluded from that build. Select it directly to compile it.

### Root files inside source directories

A source directory recognises exactly three optional root files: `@mod.moth`, `@package.moth` and `@entry.moth`. The directory may contain any present subset. One directory forms one structural module with shared helper files.

`@mod.moth` publishes a normal API facade. `@package.moth` publishes a scoped support facade, or the external project package surface when it sits at the selected project root. `@entry.moth` owns executable page or application work. Only entry roots may hold top-level runtime code, page fragments and direct builder slots.

Any other `@*.moth` or `+*.moth` spelling is rejected as a structure error. The filename never selects a role implicitly. See @#module-roots (Module roots) for the full vocabulary and visibility rules.

A project produces an HTML page for each `@entry.moth` directory. Entry eligibility comes from file presence, not from the amount of authored activity:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    #[$in title:Welcome]
]

This metadata-only entry still produces a complete document. An empty `@entry.moth` is also eligible.

Helpers in ordinary files stay inside the surrounding module. They never become entries on their own.

### Structural packages

Project-local source-backed packages use `@package.moth`. An `@package.moth` file marks its directory as a scoped support package root named by that directory.

In the example above, `shared/@package.moth` creates the scoped `@shared` support package. It is visible to its normal-position owner module, normal sibling modules and their descendants. It is not dependency-bound from its own private implementation subtree or from another support package in the same scope.

A project-root `@package.moth` is the external project package surface. Internal project modules cannot import it. It is an ordinary support surface, not a separate graph node.

No folder gains package meaning without an `@package.moth` root. Ordinary directories do not automatically become packages.

A directory may contain both an `@mod.moth` normal root and an `@package.moth` support root. They are two isolated surfaces of one structural module. Helpers are shared, roots cannot import each other and both interfaces publish only from one successful semantic transaction.

For the full package contract see @../packages/#project-local-packages (Project-local packages).

### Current scaffold notice

`moth new html` creates a `config.moth` with an explicit valid project name, a root-level `@entry.moth` and a `.gitignore` containing `/_dev` and `/_release`. The generated config is:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    project #= (
        name = "my_site",
    )
    html #= ()
]

The generated entry contributes document metadata and a starter page through direct root inserts. Deleting the generated config is valid when the directory name is a valid project identifier.

### Source versus output

Developers own source directories. The builder generates hidden output folders and owns their stale-output cleanup. Treat generated artefacts as output, not source. A visible directory named `release` remains ordinary source.

### Builder-neutral frontend versus builder-owned output

The compiler frontend discovers modules, resolves dependencies, checks types, lowers code and validates borrowing. A project builder then decides how compiled modules become real output files. The current user-facing builder is the HTML project builder.

This separation lets the same frontend feed other builders. The frontend does not decide which modules produce artefacts. The builder owns that decision.

### Only config.moth has config semantics

No other filename has config meaning. `@config.moth` is rejected as a module-root name, not read as a config file. Alternate config filenames and compatibility handling are not supported. A nested `config.moth` is reserved and inert to the enclosing build.

### Concepts related to project layout

- For config key rules see @#project-config (Project config).
- For module root rules see @#module-roots (Module roots).
- For HTML routes and artefacts see @#html-routing-and-artifacts (HTML routing and artefacts).
